# Netlify routing. Kept in step with vercel.json — change one, change the other.
#
# This lives in public/ rather than in netlify.toml so that it ends up inside
# dist/, which is what a Netlify Drop actually uploads.

# Build output is real files or nothing. A client still holding an old chunk
# name after a deploy has to get a 404 it can recover from, never the index
# shell answered as JavaScript — that is a module parse error and a white
# screen. A file that exists is served as normal; only a miss reaches this.
/assets/*  /assets/:splat  404
/pix/*     /pix/:splat     404

# Screens are state, not routes, and the router is hash-based, so the server
# only ever sees / in normal use. This is the safety net for a stray deep link,
# which should land on the app rather than a dead end.
/*  /index.html  200
